::CW_U02_L02_Q01::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence is written in third person limited point of view?{
~A. I slipped the key under my sleeve and hoped nobody noticed.#Incorrect. This is first person because the narrator uses I and my.
~B. Dani slipped the key under her sleeve while Leo wondered why she looked nervous.#Incorrect. This enters two minds, which is not limited to one character.
=C. Dani slipped the key under her sleeve and hoped nobody noticed.#Correct. This uses Dani and her while revealing Dani's thought, so it is third person limited.
~D. The key was silver and cold beneath the light.#Incorrect. This is description, but it does not clearly establish limited access to one character's thoughts.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q02::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which question should a writer ask first when checking point of view?{
=A. Who is telling the story?#Correct. Identifying the narrator is the first step in checking point of view.
~B. How many adjectives are in the paragraph?#Incorrect. Adjectives may affect style, but they do not identify point of view first.
~C. Is the paragraph long enough?#Incorrect. Length does not determine point of view.
~D. Does the ending have a surprise?#Incorrect. Surprise may matter to plot, but the first point-of-view question is about narration.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q03::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence is first person?{
~A. She kept her hand on the envelope until the bell rang.#Incorrect. This is third person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I kept my hand on the envelope until the bell rang.#Correct. The narrator uses I and my, so the narrator is a character inside the story.
~C. Mara kept her hand on the envelope until the bell rang.#Incorrect. This is third person because the narrator uses a character's name.
~D. They kept their hands on the envelope until the bell rang.#Incorrect. This is third person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q04::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence most clearly violates first person narrator knowledge?{
~A. I saw Omar glance at the clock.#Incorrect. This reports what the narrator can observe.
~B. I thought Omar looked worried.#Incorrect. This reports the narrator's interpretation, which is allowed.
~C. I heard Omar whisper my name.#Incorrect. This reports what the narrator hears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I knew Omar regretted every word he had said.#Correct. A first person narrator cannot directly know Omar's private regret unless the story gives evidence.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q05::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which statement best explains narrative distance?{
=A. Narrative distance is how close or far the narration feels from a character's thoughts and emotions.#Correct. Distance affects how directly the reader experiences a character's inner life.
~B. Narrative distance is the number of miles between characters.#Incorrect. In this context, distance means closeness to consciousness, not physical space.
~C. Narrative distance is the length of a paragraph.#Incorrect. Paragraph length is not the definition of narrative distance.
~D. Narrative distance is the order of events in a plot.#Incorrect. Event order is sequence, not narrative distance.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q06::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which revision keeps third person limited focused on Tessa? Draft: "Tessa opened the message. Eli felt relieved because she had not seen the last line."{
~A. Tessa opened the message. Eli felt relieved because she had not seen the last line.#Incorrect. This shifts from Tessa to Eli's private feeling.
~B. Tessa opened the message. Eli thought she had not seen the last line.#Incorrect. This still enters Eli's thoughts, so it does not stay limited to Tessa.
=C. Tessa opened the message. Across the room, Eli released a slow breath when her eyes stopped before the last line.#Correct. This stays near Tessa while showing Eli's reaction through observable action.
~D. Tessa opened the message, and Eli knew she had not seen the last line.#Incorrect. This still reports Eli's knowledge. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q07::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice is an example of third person omniscient point of view?{
~A. I hid the ticket and waited by the stairs.#Incorrect. This is first person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Rae hid the ticket while Jonah wondered why the stairs suddenly felt unsafe.#Correct. The narration reveals Rae's action and Jonah's thought, showing wider knowledge.
~C. Rae hid the ticket under the stairs and held her breath.#Incorrect. This could be third person limited or objective, but it does not clearly reveal multiple minds.
~D. Under the stairs, the ticket bent at one corner.#Incorrect. This is descriptive and does not establish omniscient knowledge.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q08::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence best keeps first person point of view while showing another character's fear?{
~A. I knew Lila was terrified of what I would say.#Incorrect. This directly claims Lila's private feeling.
~B. Lila was terrified of what I would say.#Incorrect. A first person narrator cannot directly know this as fact without evidence.
~C. Lila knew I would ruin everything.#Incorrect. This directly reports Lila's private knowledge.
=D. Lila's voice cracked when she asked what I had heard.#Correct. The narrator observes an action and sound that suggest fear.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q09::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which passage has the clearest point of view?{
=A. I opened the locker, and Maya stepped back without saying a word.#Correct. This stays in first person by reporting observable behavior.
~B. I opened the locker, and Maya wondered why I looked guilty.#Incorrect. This begins in first person but enters Maya's mind.
~C. I opened the locker, and Maya was secretly angry.#Incorrect. This reports Maya's private emotion from first person.
~D. I opened the locker, and Maya knew the truth.#Incorrect. This reports Maya's private knowledge. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q10::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] How does first person point of view often affect a story?{
~A. It always reveals every character's thoughts.#Incorrect. First person usually limits the reader to the narrator's knowledge.
=B. It can create closeness because the narrator tells the story from inside the experience.#Correct. First person can feel personal because readers hear the narrator's own voice.
~C. It removes narrator voice.#Incorrect. First person often creates a strong narrator voice.
~D. It prevents writers from using narrative techniques.#Incorrect. Writers can use action, dialogue, description, and reflection in first person.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q11::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which evidence best shows that a passage is first person?{
~A. The passage includes a setting.#Incorrect. Any point of view can include setting.
~B. The passage includes dialogue.#Incorrect. Any point of view can include dialogue.
=C. The narrator uses I, me, or my.#Correct. First person is usually signaled by first person pronouns.
~D. The passage has a surprise ending.#Incorrect. Plot structure does not identify first person.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q12::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence best shows third person limited with close narrative distance?{
=A. Mira stared at the clock and wished she could stop the minute hand with her eyes.#Correct. The sentence stays with Mira and reveals her inner wish.
~B. The room was square and quiet.#Incorrect. This is distant description and does not enter a character's experience.
~C. People moved through the room.#Incorrect. This is broad and distant. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A clock hung above the door.#Incorrect. This is description without inner access. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q13::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which feedback best helps a peer revise a point-of-view problem?{
~A. This is bad.#Incorrect. This is too vague to help revision.
~B. Add more adjectives.#Incorrect. Adjectives do not solve the point-of-view problem.
~C. Make it longer.#Incorrect. Length does not fix narrator knowledge. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. This starts in first person, but the second sentence directly reports another character's thoughts. Revise by showing that character's feeling through action or dialogue.#Correct. This feedback names the issue and gives a precise revision path.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q14::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice correctly describes third person limited?{
~A. A character narrates using I.#Incorrect. That describes first person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The narrator uses third person pronouns while staying close to one character's experience.#Correct. That is the core of third person limited.
~C. The narrator has no access to any character's thoughts.#Incorrect. Third person limited can access one focus character's thoughts.
~D. The narrator reveals every character's private thoughts equally.#Incorrect. That is closer to omniscient narration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q15::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence is most likely third person omniscient?{
~A. I held the photograph against my chest.#Incorrect. This is first person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Leah held the photograph and tried not to cry.#Incorrect. This is close to Leah only, so it is third person limited.
=C. Leah held the photograph while Sam, across town, wondered why she had stopped calling.#Correct. The narrator reveals knowledge connected to more than one character/location.
~D. The photograph curled at the edge.#Incorrect. This does not reveal any character's mind.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q16::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which revision best changes first person to third person limited? Original: "I waited outside the office and wished I had told the truth earlier."{
=A. Marcus waited outside the office and wished he had told the truth earlier.#Correct. This uses third person while staying close to Marcus's thoughts.
~B. I waited outside the office and wished I had told the truth earlier.#Incorrect. This remains first person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Marcus waited outside the office, and Ms. Li knew he had lied.#Incorrect. This enters another character's knowledge, so it is not limited to Marcus.
~D. Waiting happened outside the office.#Incorrect. This removes the character and does not create clear third person limited narration.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q17::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Draft: "Ava opened the notebook. She wondered who had written in it. Across the room, Ben knew the answer." Why is this point-of-view draft confusing?{
~A. It has no character names.#Incorrect. It includes Ava and Ben. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It has no action.#Incorrect. Ava opens the notebook. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It uses first person pronouns.#Incorrect. It uses third person pronouns and names.
=D. It begins close to Ava's thoughts, then suddenly enters Ben's knowledge.#Correct. That creates a point-of-view shift unless the writer is intentionally using controlled omniscient narration.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q18::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which choice best explains why point of view affects reader trust?{
~A. Readers trust every narrator equally.#Incorrect. Narrators vary in knowledge, honesty, and interpretation.
=B. The narrator controls what information the reader receives and how it is framed.#Correct. Point of view shapes access to information and interpretation.
~C. Point of view removes voice from a story.#Incorrect. Point of view strongly affects voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Point of view is only about punctuation.#Incorrect. Point of view is about perspective and knowledge.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q19::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence best keeps third person limited focused on Devon?{
~A. Devon heard the drawer open, and Mia hoped he would not turn around.#Incorrect. This enters Mia's thoughts too. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Devon heard the drawer open, and Mia knew he was afraid.#Incorrect. This enters Mia's knowledge. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Devon heard the drawer open behind him and wished he had locked it.#Correct. The sentence uses third person and reveals Devon's thought only.
~D. Devon heard the drawer open, and everyone in the house felt nervous.#Incorrect. This makes a broad claim about everyone's feelings.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q20::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which point of view is used here? "We waited behind the curtain until the principal called our names."{
=A. First person#Correct. We and our are first person plural pronouns.
~B. Third person limited#Incorrect. The narrator uses we and our, not third person pronouns.
~C. Third person omniscient#Incorrect. The passage does not use third person or reveal multiple minds.
~D. Objective third person#Incorrect. The pronouns show first person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q21::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence creates the closest narrative distance?{
~A. The school was built in 1978.#Incorrect. This is distant factual description. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I pressed my schedule flat and tried to remember how to breathe.#Correct. This places the reader close to the narrator's physical and emotional experience.
~C. Several students walked into the school.#Incorrect. This is broad and external. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The front doors opened at 7:10.#Incorrect. This is distant and factual. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q22::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which sentence best avoids directly naming another character's private thought in first person?{
~A. I knew Theo regretted giving me the card.#Incorrect. This directly claims Theo's private regret.
~B. Theo regretted giving me the card.#Incorrect. This directly reports Theo's private thought.
~C. Theo knew I would keep the card forever.#Incorrect. This directly reports Theo's private knowledge.
=D. Theo stared at the card in my hand and rubbed the corner of the envelope.#Correct. This gives observable evidence that the first person narrator can report.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q23::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which statement is true about first person narration?{
~A. It automatically gives access to every character's mind.#Incorrect. First person does not automatically know other minds.
~B. It cannot include dialogue.#Incorrect. First person narratives can include dialogue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. It can be limited because the narrator only knows their own thoughts and observations.#Correct. First person often creates closeness but limits direct knowledge.
~D. It cannot include description.#Incorrect. First person narratives can include description. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q24::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which revision best fixes the shift? Draft: "I followed Carlos into the gym. He hoped nobody would notice the envelope."{
~A. I followed Carlos into the gym. He hoped nobody would notice the envelope.#Incorrect. This keeps the direct claim about Carlos's private hope.
~B. I followed Carlos into the gym, and he hoped privately.#Incorrect. This still reports Carlos's private hope.
~C. Carlos hoped nobody would notice the envelope while I followed him.#Incorrect. This shifts away from the first person narrator and still reports Carlos's private thought.
=D. I followed Carlos into the gym. He kept one hand over the envelope and avoided looking at the ticket table.#Correct. This keeps first person by reporting observable behavior.
}

::CW_U02_L02_Q25::[MLA.CW.NAR.01] Which explanation best connects point of view to voice?{
=A. Point of view shapes voice because the narrator's position affects word choice, attitude, knowledge, and distance.#Correct. The narrator's perspective influences how the story sounds and what it can reveal.
~B. Point of view and voice are unrelated.#Incorrect. Point of view strongly affects voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Voice only matters in speeches, not stories.#Incorrect. Voice matters in written narrative. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Point of view eliminates tone.#Incorrect. Point of view helps create tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

